# The question

How should the cluster's own applications authenticate to a CNPG database? Humans are out of scope — interactive access rides peer auth in the pods or the pg-oauth developer SSO — this map is only the app pools. Neither option is a thesis to defend here — each criterion below collects what both sides have to say, and the context section names the conditions that swing the verdict. Node color marks which side an argument serves; the arrows carry the valence.

[Passwords]: Keep scram-sha-256 passwords — the app role's credential is a string generated once, stored in a sops-encrypted Secret for the Cluster's passwordSecret, copied into each consumer's helm_secrets, and checked by Postgres's default auth path. #passwords
  -> [Client certs]

[Client certs]: Authenticate with mTLS client certificates — cert-manager issues each app a certificate whose CN is the Postgres role, signed by a private client CA the Cluster trusts via clientCASecret, and possession of the mounted key is the login; no password exists. #certs
  -> [Passwords]


# Secret distribution and rotation

<Copied everywhere it's used>: A password must exist verbatim in every place that uses it — the database's passwordSecret plus one sops file per consuming app — so every consumer added is another encrypted copy to keep in step, and every rotation is a coordinated multi-file change that must land everywhere at once or something breaks. #certs
  +> [Client certs]
  - <sops already solved custody>: The secret pipeline this repo runs anyway makes each copy encrypted at rest, reviewed in PRs, and stamped out by the same tooling as every other secret — one more key in helm_secrets is zero new machinery, and "coordinated change" is one commit that Flux rolls out. #passwords

<Rotation is issuance>: Certificates renew themselves — cert-manager reissues on a schedule against a stable CA keypair, per app, with no coordination between consumers and no server-side change, so the rotation discipline teams perpetually defer becomes a property of the machinery instead of a calendar entry. #certs
  +> [Client certs]
  - <Renewal lands only at pod restart>: The app reads the key file once when it builds its connection config, so a renewed secret reaches a long-lived pod only on its next restart — a pod that outlives the renewal margin fails new connections with a TLS error passwords cannot produce, and the mitigation (long lifetimes, or restart-on-renewal tooling) must be designed, not assumed. #passwords

# Blast radius and revocation

<One string is every app's access>: A shared password leaked from any single copy — one app's env, one mis-scoped sops rule, one debug dump — opens the database as the app role from anywhere with network reach, and the response is an everything-at-once rotation fire drill across all consumers simultaneously. #certs
  +> [Client certs]
  - <Shared CN, shared blast radius>: When every app's certificate carries the same role CN, a stolen key grants exactly what the stolen password did — the same role, the same tables — so per-app issuance narrows which app must re-issue, not what the attacker reached. #passwords
    - <Containment is still per-app>: Response cost is the point: one app's cert is reissued in minutes without touching nine healthy neighbours, the leak is attributable to the workload that held that serial, and per-app CNs with a pg_ident map — a small step from the shared-CN starting point — narrow the reach too. #certs

<Postgres speaks no CRL>: Revocation is where the cert story is weakest — Postgres consults no CRL or OCSP, so a stolen key works until its cert expires, and truly cutting it off early means rotating the client CA, the most disruptive PKI operation there is; a password can be changed server-side in one statement, instantly invalidating every copy. #passwords
  +> [Passwords]
  - <Short lifetimes are the revocation>: Certificate lifetime is chosen, not suffered — automated renewal makes short validity nearly free, bounding a stolen key's usefulness by policy, while the "instant" password reset is instant only server-side: every legitimate consumer breaks that instant too, until the new value is distributed everywhere the old one lived. #certs

# Exposure surface

<A bearer secret travels>: The password crosses a boundary at every use — it sits in process env where crash dumps, /proc, spawned children, and over-eager log frameworks can capture it, and anyone who captures it can replay it from anywhere; a private key never leaves its file, never crosses the wire, and possession must be re-proven cryptographically at every handshake. #certs
  +> [Client certs]

# Drivers and operational fit

<Every client speaks password>: scram is the lingua franca — every driver, every packaged third-party chart, every GUI tool authenticates with it out of the box — while client certs are supported almost everywhere but uneven in the details: key-file permission rules differ per driver, mounted-file ownership must match container users, and the failures surface at the TLS layer where far fewer people debug fluently. #passwords
  +> [Passwords]
  - <The sharp edges are one-time>: File modes, ownership, and env plumbing are solved once in the template and stamped out identically for every app thereafter, while the password's tax — distribution, rotation, custody — recurs for every app and every rotation, forever; a one-time setup cost against a perpetual operating cost. #certs

<PKI joins the critical path>: The CA and cert-manager become availability dependencies of database authentication — an expired CA, a wedged issuer, or a botched cert-manager upgrade now blocks logins — where a password needs nothing but the database itself to keep working. #passwords
  +> [Passwords]
  - <Only new connections, with weeks of margin>: An issuer outage threatens issuance, not sessions — established connections keep working, renewals land weeks before expiry, and the pieces at risk are components this template already operates and monitors for its gateway TLS rather than new machinery adopted for this purpose. #certs

<Rehearsable before the flip>: The migration is verifiable in advance — apps can present certificates while still authenticating by password, and pg_stat_ssl shows exactly which connections carry a client cert — so the pg_hba cutover happens only after every consumer has demonstrably proven itself, where a password rotation's mismatch window can only be observed as errors while it happens. #certs
  +> [Client certs]

# Cross-cutting context

[Third-party consumers in the mix]: Packaged apps you don't build often cannot present a client certificate at all — their charts know only a password field — so a fleet with such consumers stays mixed-auth regardless, and this map governs the first-party apps only. #context
  +> <Every client speaks password>

[cert-manager is already table stakes]: This template deploys cert-manager unconditionally for gateway TLS, so the PKI "new dependency" is largely a sunk cost — the marginal machinery is one CA Certificate and one ClusterIssuer, not a new platform component. #context
  +> <Only new connections, with weeks of margin>
  -> <PKI joins the critical path>

[Postgres 18 OAuth is on the horizon]: PG 18 ships native OAuth (this template's pg-oauth block already uses it for humans), but application drivers do not yet speak OAUTHBEARER — when they do, both options face the same successor, which shortens the payback window of any migration undertaken now. #context
  +> [Automate the password instead]


# Middle paths

[Automate the password instead]: Keep scram and make one system own the credential — external-secrets sourcing a cloud secrets manager (or its password generator) pushes the same value to the database's passwordSecret and every consumer, rotated on a schedule — the smallest architectural change that still retires the hand-managed copies. #alternative
  -> [Passwords]
  -> [Client certs]
  + <No app changes at all>: Rotation automation is purely infrastructure-side — no driver plumbing, no file modes, no TLS-layer failure modes — and it composes with any consumer that takes a password, third-party charts included. #alternative
  - <Rotation is not elimination>: The credential is still a bearer secret present at runtime in every consumer; automation shrinks the window between leak and rotation but keeps the class of leak alive, where certificates remove the replayable secret rather than refreshing it faster. #certs

[Dynamic credentials]: Run Vault or OpenBao and mint short-lived, per-app database roles on demand — apps authenticate with their ServiceAccount identity and nothing static exists to steal — the strongest model on this map. #alternative
  -> [Passwords]
  -> [Client certs]
  - <A vault is a platform>: The price is operating a secrets platform on the database's critical path — unseal management, HA, upgrades, and a client handshake taught to every app — an adoption that dwarfs both options it replaces and is rarely justified by database auth alone. #passwords
